amp_bao: fix Bao self-coloring boot hang (I-cache coherency on relocate)

Enabling hypervisor self-coloring (config.hyp.colors, BAO_HYP_COLORING=1)
hangs Bao before any UART output, while guest-only coloring works. Root
cause: the EL2 self-relocation resumes execution from a freshly relocated
copy of .text without any instruction-cache coherency.

mem_color_hypervisor() -> copy_space() relocates the hypervisor image with a
data-side memcpy only, then switch_space() swaps TTBR0_EL2 and returns into
the copied image. On a system with caches enabled this violates the ARM
self-modifying/relocated-code contract: the copied instructions may still be
in the D-cache (not yet at the PoU/PoC) and stale I-cache lines for the
colored physical pages may be present, so the post-switch fetch executes
garbage and hangs. The only existing maintenance, cache_flush_range() after
switch_space(), is both too late (execution already resumed) and D-side only
(dc civac, no ic).

Fix, split across the two relocation stages:
  - mem.c: CPU_MASTER cleans the freshly copied image to the PoC (via the
    temporary copy VA) right after copy_space(), before the sync barrier and
    the TTBR switch, so the relocated instructions are in memory.
  - relocate.S: each PE invalidates its I-cache (ic iallu) in switch_space()
    just before writing TTBR0_EL2, so the resume fetches the fresh image.

No effect on default builds: mem_color_hypervisor() only runs when
config.hyp.colors is set (HYP_COLORS=0 => all_clrs() => not invoked). This is
one of the two required functional fixes for hyp self-coloring (the other is
0009); the optional g_hyp_color_stage breadcrumb that helped confirm it on
hardware now lives in the standalone debug patch 0010.

diff --git a/src/arch/armv8/armv8-a/aarch64/relocate.S b/src/arch/armv8/armv8-a/aarch64/relocate.S
index cf45a2a..54aaa7c 100644
--- a/src/arch/armv8/armv8-a/aarch64/relocate.S
+++ b/src/arch/armv8/armv8-a/aarch64/relocate.S
@@ -90,6 +90,20 @@ switch_space:
     dsb ish
     isb
 
+    /**
+     * Invalidate the instruction cache before resuming from the relocated
+     * image. switch_space() returns into the recolored copy of .text: the same
+     * VAs are about to map to the colored physical pages. That copy was made by
+     * a data-side memcpy in copy_space() and cleaned to the PoC by
+     * mem_color_hypervisor() before this call, so the bytes are in memory; here
+     * each PE drops any stale I-cache lines for those physical pages so the
+     * post-switch fetch hits the freshly relocated instructions. Without this
+     * the resume fetches stale instructions and hangs before any UART output.
+     */
+    ic iallu
+    dsb ish
+    isb
+
     /**
      * Update TTBR
      */
diff --git a/src/core/mmu/mem.c b/src/core/mmu/mem.c
index 7145786..2b1cca6 100644
--- a/src/core/mmu/mem.c
+++ b/src/core/mmu/mem.c
@@ -804,7 +804,20 @@ void mem_color_hypervisor(const paddr_t load_addr, struct mem_region* root_regio
      * to access it.
      */
     if (cpu_is_master()) {
-        copy_space(&_image_start, image_size, &p_image);
+        void* image_cpy = copy_space(&_image_start, image_size, &p_image);
+        /*
+         * copy_space() relocates the hypervisor image (including .text) with a
+         * data-side memcpy only. Clean the freshly written copy to the PoC so
+         * the relocated instructions are visible to instruction fetch once
+         * switch_space() repoints TTBR0_EL2 at these colored pages; otherwise
+         * the post-switch resume fetches stale memory and hangs before
+         * console_init(). Flush via the temporary copy VA because the linked VA
+         * still maps to the old image at this point. CPU_MASTER-only is enough:
+         * the PoC is system-wide, and the per-PE I-cache invalidate lives in
+         * switch_space(). (The existing flush after switch_space() runs too late
+         * — execution has already resumed from these pages.)
+         */
+        cache_flush_range((vaddr_t)image_cpy, image_size);
         va = mem_alloc_vpage(&cpu_new->as, SEC_HYP_IMAGE, (vaddr_t)&_image_start,
             NUM_PAGES(image_size));
 
